  "coverage": [
    "Substring of namespaced ID and color-stripped display name, case folding, spaces and literal four lines, unrelated category/material/lore exclusion, 45-entry pagination, empty matches and blank query.",
    "Main and category compass, aliases, per-viewer permissions, hidden entries, category-independent search, canonical grant and denied clicks, non-inspectable results, recipe Back preserves query/page, search Back and Close constraints.",
    "Multiple simultaneous players, immediate permission revocation, delayed responses after a typed command, independent inventory and successful definition reload, disconnect and clean reconnect; owner-only editor and restoration of live tile data.",
    "Dependency-free typed search and exact English fallback after command and button, with no synthetic sign/editor traffic."
  ],
  "method": "Two independent headless native clients sent real commands, container clicks and four-line sign submissions from an editor each had received. The probe compared every top-inventory stack with decoded native item identities, watched the second player in the same chunk, inspected server block data and sentinels, and recorded private fake-block, editor, original block-state and tile-entity packets. Official version-matched ProtocolLib builds were present in registered adapter cases only. Each isolated process, server, client and input manifest was verified after shutdown.",
  "limits": "Rendered sign editor and inventory appearance are reserved for the user. The logout case does not require a restore packet after the client has disconnected. The first Paper 26.3 run passed all server assertions. Its private validation-client NBT reader mishandled integer arrays in unrelated join and quit chat, so the matrix uses a repeated run after the client reader was corrected. The earlier Spigot 1.21.11 pilot used Prismarine for this codec, was unaffected, and had no native decode errors.",
